[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186364599 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above, where ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set of vectors. You're given that $‖{v}_{1}‖=1,$ $‖{v}_{2}‖=3,$ $‖{v}_{3}‖=2,$ and that the system of equations below describes the relationship between the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$ \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}={a}_{2}-3{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3}={a}_{3}-{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{12}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}?$"] [17592186364599 :question/worked-solution "First, we normalize the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{1}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{1}={q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{3}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{2}=3{q}_{2} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{2}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{3}=2{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set ${{q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.\n\nNext, let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}={a}_{2}-3{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3}={a}_{3}-{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}={v}_{1}={q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}=3{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=3{q}_{1}+3{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}={v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}={q}_{1}+6{q}_{2}+2{q}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{12}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}=3+3+6=12.\n$$"] [17592186364606 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above, where ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set of vectors. You're given that $‖{v}_{1}‖=6,$ $‖{v}_{2}‖=3,$ $‖{v}_{3}‖=2,$ and that the system of equations below describes the relationship between the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$ \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}={a}_{2}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3}={a}_{3}-2{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{22}+{r}_{23}+{r}_{33}?$"] [17592186364606 :question/worked-solution "First, we normalize the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{6}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{1}=6{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{3}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{2}=3{q}_{2} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{2}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{3}=2{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set ${{q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.\n\nNext, let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}={a}_{2}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3}={a}_{3}-2{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}={v}_{1}=6{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}=-{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=-6{q}_{1}+3{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}=2{v}_{1}-{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=12{q}_{1}-3{q}_{2}+2{q}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 & -6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}+{r}_{33}=3+(-3)+2=2.\n$$"] [17592186364613 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{12}?$"] [17592186364613 :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{12}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}}\\cdot 0=0.$"] [17592186364620 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}.$"] [17592186364620 :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{6}{18})[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}=(\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (3\\sqrt{2})=4.$"] [17592186364627 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}.$"] [17592186364627 :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{3}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}=(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}})\\cdot (\\sqrt{6})=-2.$"] [17592186364681 :question/problem "The function $f(x,y)$ has Hessian matrix\n\n$$\n{∇}^{2}f(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2(y-3) & 2(x+1) \\\\ 2(x+1) & (y-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(-1,3).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $H(-1,3)<0$\n- **II.** $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,3)>0$\n- **III.** The second partial derivative test is inconclusive"] [17592186364681 :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ then:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$;\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a.$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a.$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivative test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the Hessian at $(-1,3),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∇}^{2}f(-1,3)=[\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{2}+2(3-3) & 2(-1+1) \\\\ 2(-1+1) & (3-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The Hessian determinant is\n\n$$\nH(-1,3)=det({∇}^{2}f(-1,3))=1(0)-0(0)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true because\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,3)=1>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true because $H(-1,3)=0,$ so the second partial derivative test is inconclusive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186364688 :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4x+4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4x+4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x-4 & 6y\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-4=0 \\\\ 6y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n2x-4=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n6y=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(2,0)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x-4)=2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6y)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-4)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6y)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(2,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2,0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(2,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(2,0)=det({f}^{″}(2,0))=2\\cdot 6-{0}^{2}=12>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,0)=({f}^{″}(2,0))_{11}=2>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(2,0).$"] [17592186364702 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B={{B}_{1},{B}_{2}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}},$ and $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$"] [17592186364702 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} & {x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1} & -3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}=4 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 11 & 11\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 6{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ 11{x}_{2}=11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second,  third and fourth equations, we get ${x}_{2}=1.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3\\cdot 1 & =1 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=(-2)+1=-1.$"] [17592186364716 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -8 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$"] [17592186364716 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -8 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & -4{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} & 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -8 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=-8 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 7\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -12 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ 6{x}_{2}=-12 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2\\cdot (-2) & =-1 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-1+4 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=3+(-2)=1.$"] [17592186364770 :question/problem "The second-order differential equation ${y}^{″}+3{e}^{2t}{y}^{′}-5y=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\\,y(t)={x}_{1}(t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $A.$"] [17592186364770 :question/worked-solution "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=-3{e}^{2t}{y}^{′}+5y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=-3{e}^{2t}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=5{x}_{1}-3{e}^{2t}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence, $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].$"] [17592186364772 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186364773 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3{e}^{2t} & 5\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186364774 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{e}^{2t} \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186364775 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -5 & 3{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186364776 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186364777 :question/problem "The third-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}+6t{y}^{″}+8{t}^{2}y=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & a \\\\ 0 & 0 & b \\\\ -8{t}^{2} & 0 & c\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand for $y=y(t),$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}={x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {y}^{″}={x}_{3}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$"] [17592186364777 :question/worked-solution "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-6t{y}^{″}-8{t}^{2}y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-6t{x}_{3}-8{t}^{2}{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=-8{t}^{2}{x}_{1}-6t{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -8{t}^{2} & 0 & -6t\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence, $a=0,b=1,c=-6t,$ and $a+b+c=1-6t.$"] [17592186364824 :question/problem "Consider the standard matrices of three linear transformations shown below. Which of the transformations map ${R}^{2}$ onto a line?\n\n$$\n{T}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,{T}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{T}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [17592186364831 :question/problem "Consider the standard matrices of three linear transformations shown below. Which of the transformations map ${R}^{2}$ onto a line?\n\n$$\n{T}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{T}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,{T}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [17592186364838 :question/problem "Consider the standard matrices of three linear transformations shown below. Which of the transformations map ${R}^{2}$ onto the whole of ${R}^{2}?$\n\n$$\n{T}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{T}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{T}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [17592186364845 :question/problem "Consider the standard matrices of three linear transformations shown below. Which of the transformations map ${R}^{2}$ onto $(0,0)?$\n\n$$\n{T}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{T}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{T}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [17592186364968 :question/problem "Given the linear transformation\n $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3y-x-4z \\\\ 2x+7z-6y \\\\ 16z+4x-12y\\end{aligned}],$ find $\\text{nullity}(T).$"] [17592186364968 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 16\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 16\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 16\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $1$ non-pivot columns (the 2nd one).  Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=1.\n$$"] [17592186364982 :question/problem "Let $B$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$, given that\n\n$\\,$ $T({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}],\\,$ $T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -13\\end{aligned}],\\,$ and $\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}.$"] [17592186364982 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 11 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 26 & -13\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 11 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186364984 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186364985 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186364986 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186364987 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186364988 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186364989 :question/problem "Consider the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ given by\n\n$$\nC=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T:V\\to {R}^{2}$ relative to these bases if \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{3})=[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 27\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186364989 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2,3.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 & 3 & 6 & 18 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -9 & -3 & 27\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -9 & -3 & 27\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -12 & -9 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -7 & -4 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -4 & 12 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [17592186364991 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -4 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186364992 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 4 & 12 \\\\ -3 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186364993 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -4 & 12 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 8\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186364994 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186364995 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -4 & 12 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186364996 :question/problem "$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSuppose $B$ and $C$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W,$ respectively. The matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:V\\to W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $x\\in V$ relative to $B$ are shown above. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$"] [17592186364996 :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}34 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186364998 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}30 \\\\ 22\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186364999 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}34 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186365000 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}28 \\\\ 32\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186365001 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}34 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186365002 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}36 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186365003 :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186365003 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365005 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186365006 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186365007 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186365008 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186365009 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186365024 :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{61}} & \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{61}} \\\\ \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{61}} & \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{61}}\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{13} & 2 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind $|\\text{det}(PQ)|$ given that $P$ is an orthogonal matrix."] [17592186365031 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the orthogonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{17} & \\frac{15}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{15}{17} & \\frac{8}{17}\\end{aligned}].$"] [17592186365031 :question/worked-solution "The inverse of an orthogonal matrix is equal to its transpose.\n\nTherefore, since $D$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{-1} & ={D}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{17} & -\\frac{15}{17} \\\\ \\frac{15}{17} & \\frac{8}{17}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365033 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{8}{17} & \\frac{15}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{15}{17} & \\frac{8}{17}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186365034 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{17} & \\frac{15}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{15}{17} & \\frac{8}{17}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186365035 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{8}{17} & \\frac{15}{17} \\\\ \\frac{15}{17} & -\\frac{8}{17}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186365037 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{17} & -\\frac{15}{17} \\\\ \\frac{15}{17} & \\frac{8}{17}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186365038 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c \\\\ d\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V=\\text{Col}(A)$ spanned by the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ of the matrix $A$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ shown above. Given that ${a}_{1}⊥{a}_{2},$ and that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$"] [17592186365038 :question/worked-solution "Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n**Step 2**: We set ${v}_{2}={a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, we find ${v}_{3}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{3}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-(0)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{(-2)}{(2)}=-1.\n$$"] [17592186365045 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c \\\\ d\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V=\\text{Col}(A)$ spanned by the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ of the matrix $A,$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ shown above. Given that ${a}_{1}⊥{a}_{2},$ and that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|\\,c\\,|?$"] [17592186365045 :question/worked-solution "Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: We set ${v}_{2}={a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, we find ${v}_{3}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{3}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{10}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{25}{5}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${v}_{3}$ dividing it by $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{10}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $|\\,c\\,|=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}.$"] [17592186365059 :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}].$"] [17592186365059 :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =2.$ Computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365061 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186365062 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186365063 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186365064 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186365065 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186365066 :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [17592186365066 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=\\sqrt{21}.\n$$"] [17592186365087 :question/worked-solution "Solving $x-y+2z=0$ for $y,$ we get $y=x+2z.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ x+2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+z{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365094 :question/worked-solution "Solving $x-3z=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=3z.$ Solving $y+w=0$ for $w,$ we get $w=-y.$  Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Ker}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3z \\\\ y \\\\ z \\\\ -y\\end{aligned}]=z{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+y{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =4-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365108 :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{3}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}-{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A-B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186365171 :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186365171 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365173 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186365174 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186365175 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186365176 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186365177 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186365178 :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5-t} \\\\ {t}^{8}-3 \\\\ {e}^{1-t}\\end{aligned}].$"] [17592186365460 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,2]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-4{y}^{3}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point $[1,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n| $[$ | $3$ | {{field-1}} | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | $4$ | $].$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} | $8$ | {{field-3}} |  |  |  |"] [17592186365460 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as\n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-4{y}^{3}-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-4{y}^{3}-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & -12{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x & 8y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & -12{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x & 8y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-4{y}^{3}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & -12{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x & 8y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-4{y}^{3}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3(1)^{2} & -12(1)^{2} \\\\ 2(1) & 8(1)\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}(1)^{3}-4(1)^{3}-1 \\\\ (1)^{2}+4(1)^{2}-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & -12 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365467 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,0]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-7 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point $[-1,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n| $[$ | $-6$ | $2$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | {{field-1}} | $].$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | $3$ |  |  |  |"] [17592186365467 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as\n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-7) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-7) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-8) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-8)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6x & 2y \\\\ 2x & 8y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}6x & 2y \\\\ 2x & 8y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-7 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}6x & 2y \\\\ 2x & 8y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-7 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6(-1) & 2(1) \\\\ 2(-1) & 8(1)\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}3(-1)^{2}+(1)^{2}-7 \\\\ (-1)^{2}+4(1)^{2}-8\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365474 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,2]\\times [1,3]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y-4y+6 \\\\ xy-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point  $[0,\\,1]^{T},$ and compute the components of the first iteration.\n\n| $[$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | {{field-3}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $2$ |  |  |  |\n\n| ${x}_{1}=[$ | $2$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [17592186365474 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-4y+6) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-4y+6) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2xy & {x}^{2}-4 \\\\ y & x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}2xy & {x}^{2}-4 \\\\ y & x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y-4y+6 \\\\ xy-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}2xy & {x}^{2}-4 \\\\ y & x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y-4y+6 \\\\ xy-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2(0)(1) & {0}^{2}-4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2}(1)-4(1)+6 \\\\ (0)(1)-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0.5\\end{aligned}].$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0.5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0.5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1.5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365483 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,2]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-xy-4 \\\\ {x}^{4}-2xy+{y}^{2}-5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[0,\\,1]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[-1.5,\\,1.5]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}\\,≈[$ | $-1.198$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-1}} |  |  |"] [17592186365483 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-xy-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-xy-4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-2xy+{y}^{2}-5) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-2xy+{y}^{2}-5)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x-y & 3{y}^{2}-x \\\\ 4{x}^{3}-2y & -2x+2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x-y & 3{y}^{2}-x \\\\ 4{x}^{3}-2y & -2x+2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-xy-4 \\\\ {x}^{4}-2xy+{y}^{2}-5\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x-y & 3{y}^{2}-x \\\\ 4{x}^{3}-2y & -2x+2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-xy-4 \\\\ {x}^{4}-2xy+{y}^{2}-5\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.5 \\\\ 1.5\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(-1.5)-1.5 & 3(1.5)^{2}-(-1.5) \\\\ 4(-1.5)^{3}-2(1.5) & -2(-1.5)+2(1.5)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(-1.5)^{2}+{1.5}^{3}-(-1.5)\\cdot 1.5-4 \\\\ (-1.5)^{4}-2(-1.5)(1.5)+{1.5}^{2}-5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.5 \\\\ 1.5\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-4.5 & 8.25 \\\\ -16.5 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3.875 \\\\ 6.8125\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.5 \\\\ 1.5\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{-4.5\\cdot 6-8.25\\cdot (-16.5)}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -8.25 \\\\ 16.5 & -4.5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3.875 \\\\ 6.8125\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.5 \\\\ 1.5\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{109.125}[\\begin{aligned}-32.953\\,125 \\\\ 33.281\\,250\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-1.198 \\\\ 1.195\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [17592186365486 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-3,-1]\\times [-4,-1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+xy+{y}^{2}-12 \\\\ {x}^{3}+x-y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,-1]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[-1.4,\\,-3.6]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [17592186365486 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+xy+{y}^{2}-12) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xy+{y}^{2}-12) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+x-y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+x-y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x+y & x+2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x+y & x+2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+xy+{y}^{2}-12 \\\\ {x}^{3}+x-y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x+y & x+2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+xy+{y}^{2}-12 \\\\ {x}^{3}+x-y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.4 \\\\ -3.6\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(-1.4)+(-3.6) & (-1.4)+2(-3.6) \\\\ 3(-1.4)^{2}+1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(-1.4)^{2}+(-1.4)(-3.6)+(-3.6)^{2}-12 \\\\ (-1.4)^{3}+(-1.4)-(-3.6)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.4 \\\\ -3.6\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-6.4 & -8.6 \\\\ 6.88 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}7.96 \\\\ -0.544\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.4 \\\\ -3.6\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-6.4)\\cdot (-1)-(-8.6)\\cdot (6.88)}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 8.6 \\\\ -6.88 & -6.4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7.96 \\\\ -0.544\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.4 \\\\ -3.6\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{65.568}[\\begin{aligned}-12.6384 \\\\ -51.2832\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-1.207 \\\\ -2.818\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [17592186365498 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ Find ${A}^{3}.$\n\n| ${A}^{3}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [17592186365498 :question/worked-solution "First, to find ${A}^{2},$ we multiply $A$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 20 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${A}^{3}={A}^{2}\\cdot A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 20 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-20 & -15 \\\\ 60 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365507 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $E=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ Find ${E}^{2}.$\n\n| ${E}^{2}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $2$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-4$ | {{field-3}} | $-2$ |  |  |\n|  | {{field-4}} | $0$ | {{field-5}} |  |  |"] [17592186365507 :question/worked-solution "To find ${E}^{2},$ we multiply $E$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}^{2} & =E\\cdot E \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & 2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365518 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ Find ${A}^{2}.$\n\n| ${A}^{2}=\\,\\,$ |  | $3$ | $-3$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | $0$ |  |  |\n|  | {{field-4}} | $11$ | {{field-5}} |  |  |"] [17592186365518 :question/worked-solution "To find ${A}^{2},$ we multiply $A$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 11 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365529 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ If $f(X)={X}^{2}+X-4$, find $f(B).$\n\n| $f(B)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [17592186365529 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}+X-4 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}+X-4I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(B)={B}^{2}+B-4I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${B}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(B):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(B) & ={B}^{2}+B-4I \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2+0-4 & 6+2-0 \\\\ -3+(-1)-0 & 7+3-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365538 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $E=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ If $f(X)={X}^{2}+4X-5$, find $f(E).$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$f(E)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-3}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">\n{{field-4}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186365538 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}+4X-5 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}+4X-5I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(E)={E}^{2}+4E-5I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${E}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}^{2} & =E\\cdot E \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(E):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(E) & ={E}^{2}+4E-5I \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4+8-5 & 0+0-0 \\\\ 3+4-0 & 1+4-5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365547 :question/problem "Given that $B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & k+2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 6\\end{aligned}]$ and $det(B)=70$, find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186365550 :question/problem "Find, in **ascending order**, the values ​​of $x$ for which the following matrix is singular.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}x+2 & 2x & 4 \\\\ 3 & x & x+1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIn ascending order, $x=$\n{{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}."] [17592186365603 :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n- **I.** ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- **II.** ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- **III.** ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186365610 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]x(t).$"] [17592186365610 :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{7t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"] [17592186365612 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"] [17592186365613 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{7t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"] [17592186365614 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{7t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"] [17592186365615 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{7t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R"] [17592186365616 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R"] [17592186365620 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186365620 :question/worked-solution "We have a decoupled system. Therefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-6t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{3}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-6t}+(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{3}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-6t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{3}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365622 :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{3}t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-6t}"] [17592186365623 :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-6t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{3}t}"] [17592186365624 :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-6t}"] [17592186365625 :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{3}t}"] [17592186365626 :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-6t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{3}t}"] [17592186365659 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>"] [17592186365659 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & -6{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 6{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=6\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-6\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((6\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(6\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(6\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+6\\text{i}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ 6{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{6t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ 6{\\alpha }_{1}t & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{6t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186365668 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">${e}^{5t}-{e}^{-5t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">${e}^{5t}-{e}^{-5t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>"] [17592186365668 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & 5{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-5t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=5t,\n$$\n\neach of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}r(-5t)={e}^{-5t} \\\\ r(5t)={e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-5t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-5t} \\\\ 5t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtracting the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-10t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-5t}-{e}^{5t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{10t}({e}^{-5t}-{e}^{5t}).\n$$\n\n- Adding the first and the second equations, we get\n\n$$\n2{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-5t}+{e}^{5t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-5t}+{e}^{5t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-5t}+{e}^{5t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{5t}+{e}^{-5t}) \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =5\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{10t}({e}^{-5t}-{e}^{5t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{5t}-{e}^{-5t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t}+{e}^{-5t} & {e}^{5t}-{e}^{-5t} \\\\ {e}^{5t}-{e}^{-5t} & {e}^{5t}+{e}^{-5t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186365673 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| ${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [17592186365673 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 0 & -4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-4t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(-4t)={e}^{-4t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(-4t)={e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}-4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-4t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-4t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-4t{e}^{-4t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-4t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-4t}(1+4t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-4{e}^{-4t}t+{e}^{-4t}(1+4t) \\\\  & ={e}^{-4t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & ={e}^{-4t}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-4t} & t{e}^{-4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186365731 :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L3}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent solutions of the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ This means that each column satisfies the differential equation. In particular, \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from the fact that ${x}_{2}\\text{ is a solution of the system}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L4}$ and $\\text{L5}.$ By definition, the derivative of $Φ(t)$ is obtained by differentiating each column,\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}^{′}(t) & {x}_{2}^{′}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{1}(t)$ from $\\text{L2}$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t)$ from $\\text{L3},$ we substitute each derivative into the matrix to get\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ A{x}_{1}(t) & A{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L4}$ by substitution from $\\text{L2}\\text{ and }\\text{L3}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L6}$ and $\\text{L7}.$ From $\\text{L6}$ we have\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=A[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)].\n$$\n\nUsing the definition $Φ(t)=[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)]$ from $\\text{L1},$ we substitute to obtain\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=AΦ(t).\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L7}$ follows from $\\text{L6}$ by substitution from $\\text{L1}.$"] [17592186365792 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & -2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2{e}^{t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{2}{3}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"60px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186365792 :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & -2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-t}\\cdot 2{e}^{3t}-(-2{e}^{3t})\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{3t} & 2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{3t} & 2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -2{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & -2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365800 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}5t \\\\ 7t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ |  | $8t-14$ |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  |  |"] [17592186365800 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & 2{e}^{2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5t \\\\ 7t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-t}\\cdot {e}^{2t}-2{e}^{2t}\\cdot 2{e}^{-t}}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -2{e}^{2t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{3}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -2{e}^{2t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t} & -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t} & -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5t \\\\ 7t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}3t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 3t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int t{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=3t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=3\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v={e}^{t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(3t)\\cdot {e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =3t{e}^{t}-3{e}^{t} \\\\  & =3{e}^{t}(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=1\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(t)\\cdot (-\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{2})-\\int (-\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{2})\\cdot (1\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{t{e}^{-2t}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{4}(2t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & 2{e}^{2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t}(t-1) \\\\ -\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{4}(2t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}12(t-1)-2(2t+1) \\\\ 24(t-1)-(2t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}8t-14 \\\\ 22t-25\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365803 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{t} \\\\ 3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & {e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{3t} & -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{p}(t)={e}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"60px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2{e}^{3t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"60px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{-19}{6}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186365803 :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & {e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{3t} & -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{t} \\\\ 3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-{e}^{-2t})-{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{3t}}[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-2t} & -{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{3t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-2t} & -{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{3t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{t} \\\\ 3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 5{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int 2{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & {e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{3t} & -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{t} \\\\ -\\frac{19}{6}{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{19}{6}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186365808 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$ |  | $-2t-3$ |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  |  |"] [17592186365808 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{t}-(-{e}^{t})\\cdot 2{e}^{-2t}}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{3}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{3}t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{3}t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -\\frac{8}{3}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=\\frac{4}{3}t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{4}{3}t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}\\cdot (\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}t{e}^{2t}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int {e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}t{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{2t}(2t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=-\\frac{8}{3}t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-{e}^{-t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{8}{3}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-\\frac{8}{3}t\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})-\\int -{e}^{-t}\\cdot (-\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}t{e}^{-t}-\\frac{8}{3}\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}t{e}^{-t}+\\frac{8}{3}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{e}^{-t}(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{2t}(2t-1) \\\\ \\frac{8}{3}{e}^{-t}(t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}(2t-1)-8(t+1) \\\\ 2(2t-1)+8(t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2t-3 \\\\ 4t+2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186366006 :question/problem "$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -7 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $C$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C$\n\n- **II.** $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C$\n\n- **III.** The eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$"] [17592186366006 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,C-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nC-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & -7 & 6 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(C-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4\\times 4$ determinant across the $4$th column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & -7 & 6 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3\\times 3$ determinant across the $2$nd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5-\\lambda )\\cdot (5-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ -5 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )^{2}\\cdot ((2-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )+20) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)^{2}({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +14) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D<0$ for the polynomial ${\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +14$ we have that the only real eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (with algebraic multiplicity $2$). \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186366013 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 4 & 5 & -12 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -7\\end{aligned}].$"] [17592186366013 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,C-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nC-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 3 & -6 \\\\ 4 & 5-\\lambda  & -12 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(C-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 3 & -6 \\\\ 4 & 5-\\lambda  & -12 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & -12 \\\\ 3 & -7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}4 & -12 \\\\ 3 & -7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|+(-6)|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5-\\lambda  \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-3(-4\\lambda +8)-6(-3+3\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (-{\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +4)-(-12\\lambda +24)-(-18+18\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186366021 :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]}"] [17592186366022 :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] [17592186366023 :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] [17592186366024 :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}"] [17592186366025 :answer/value "{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] [17592186366026 :question/problem "Let $B$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$, given that\n\n$\\,$ $T({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,$ $T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,$ and $\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\}.$"] [17592186366026 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 5 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -7 & -18\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 & 18\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-3{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -18 & -46 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 & 18\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-18 & -46 \\\\ 7 & 18\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186366028 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-18 & -46 \\\\ 7 & 18\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366029 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366030 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-46 & -18 \\\\ 7 & 18\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366031 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 \\\\ 7 & 18\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366032 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ -7 & -18\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366039 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is a solution of $Tx=0$, we have that $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and therefore $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has no solution, we have that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Hence, $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"II only.\""] [17592186366073 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366074 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366075 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366076 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366077 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366078 :question/problem "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+2y \\\\ 4y+3x\\end{aligned}],$ find  $2\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)-1.$"] [17592186366078 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has no non-pivot column. Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=0.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n2\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)-1=2\\cdot 0-1=-1.\n$$"] [17592186366097 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{n}$ into ${R}^{n},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Solving $x+y=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=-y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-y \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=y{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+z{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(T)+2 & =n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T)$ then $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{n}$, so, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $T$ is not a bijection. Thus, the matrix $T$ is not invertible, and, consequently, $|T|=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=0$, then ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$ But the vectors are linearly independent, therefore, the dimension of  $\\text{Ker}(T)$ is at least $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""] [17592186366111 :question/problem "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186366111 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{12} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186366113 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366114 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366115 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366116 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366117 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366118 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$"] [17592186366118 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 2}{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis.\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthonormal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\frac{1}{3},$ $b=\\frac{2}{3},$ $c=\\frac{2}{3}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|-\\frac{1}{3}|+|\\frac{2}{3}|+|\\frac{2}{3}|=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186366125 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$"] [17592186366125 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 2}{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=4,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{0}{4}=0.\n$$"] [17592186366132 :question/problem "$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nSuppose that $V=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ Given that $B$ is an orthogonal basis for $V,$ and that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$"] [17592186366132 :question/worked-solution "We find an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{8}{8}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the vector ${v}_{3}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{6}{8}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{18}{9}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{3}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 6 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\frac{3}{2},$ $c=\\frac{3}{2},$ and $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{2})}{(\\frac{3}{2})}=-1.$"] [17592186366139 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{8}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{17}{7}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186366141 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{14}{5}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366142 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{15}{7}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366143 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{8}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{17}{7}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366144 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{13}{7}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366145 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{11}{10}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366281 :question/problem "If $\\,\\text{Re}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\text{Im}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}e & f \\\\ g & h\\end{aligned}]\\,$ are the real and imaginary parts of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2-\\text{i} & 1+3\\text{i} \\\\ -1-4\\text{i} & 2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ then what is the value of $ab+ef?$"] [17592186366281 :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2-\\text{i} & 1+3\\text{i} \\\\ -1-4\\text{i} & 2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(A)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(A)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $a=2,b=1,e=-1$ and $f=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nab+ef=2\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 3=-1.\n$$"] [17592186366288 :question/problem "If $\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-1-\\text{i} & -\\text{i} \\\\ 3 & -2+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} & -3\\text{i} \\\\ -4-\\text{i} & 6\\end{aligned}]\\,$, then $AB=$"] [17592186366288 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying the matrices using the usual method, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}-1-\\text{i} & -\\text{i} \\\\ 3 & -2+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} & -3\\text{i} \\\\ -4-\\text{i} & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(-1-\\text{i})(1+\\text{i})+(-\\text{i})(-4-\\text{i}) & (-1-\\text{i})(-3\\text{i})+(-\\text{i})\\cdot 6 \\\\ 3\\cdot (1+\\text{i})+(-2+\\text{i})(-4-\\text{i}) & 3\\cdot (-3\\text{i})+(-2+\\text{i})\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\text{i}+(4\\text{i}-1) & (3\\text{i}-3)+(-6\\text{i}) \\\\ (3+3\\text{i})+(9-2\\text{i}) & (-9\\text{i})+(-12+6\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1+2\\text{i} & -3-3\\text{i} \\\\ 12+\\text{i} & -12-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186366290 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1+2\\text{i} & -12-3\\text{i} \\\\ 12+\\text{i} & -3-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366291 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1+2\\text{i} & 12+\\text{i} \\\\ -3-3\\text{i} & -12-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366292 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12+\\text{i} & -3-3\\text{i} \\\\ -1+2\\text{i} & -12-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366293 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1+2\\text{i} & -3-3\\text{i} \\\\ 12+\\text{i} & -12-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366294 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3-12\\text{i} & -3-3\\text{i} \\\\ 12+\\text{i} & 2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366295 :question/problem "Given the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i} & 6\\text{i} \\\\ -1+3\\text{i} & -2\\end{aligned}]$, find the result of applying to $A$ the following elementary row operations: \n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(1-3\\text{i}){R}_{1}\n$$"] [17592186366295 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the row operations to $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i} & 6\\text{i} \\\\ -1+3\\text{i} & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -1+3\\text{i} & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(1-3\\text{i}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1-9\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the result  is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1-9\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186366297 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1-9\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366298 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3\\text{i} \\\\ 1 & 1-9\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366299 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366300 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 1-9\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366301 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1-9\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366302 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}].$  Find $f(B)$ if $f(X)={X}^{2}+X+3.$"] [17592186366302 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}+X+3 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}+X+3I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(B)={B}^{2}+B+3I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${B}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(B):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(B) & ={B}^{2}+B+3I \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0+2+3 & 0+1+0 \\\\ 0+(-4)+0 & 0+(-2)+3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186366304 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366305 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366306 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366307 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366308 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366309 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $H=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].$  Find $f(H)$ if $f(X)={X}^{2}+X+2.$"] [17592186366309 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}+X+2 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}+X+2I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(H)={H}^{2}+H+2I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${H}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}^{2} & =H\\cdot H \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}22 & 4 \\\\ -12 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(H):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(H) & ={H}^{2}+H+2I \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}22 & 4 \\\\ -12 & -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}22 & 4 \\\\ -12 & -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}22+5+2 & 4+1+0 \\\\ -12-3+0 & -2-1+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}29 & 5 \\\\ -15 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186366311 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 5 \\\\ -15 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366312 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}29 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366313 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}29 & 5 \\\\ -15 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366314 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}29 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366315 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ -15 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366344 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t).$"] [17592186366344 :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"] [17592186366346 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{2}\\in R"] [17592186366347 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"] [17592186366348 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"] [17592186366349 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t},\\,{c}_{2}\\in R"] [17592186366350 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"] [17592186366358 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}10x+6y \\\\ 25x+15y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$"] [17592186366358 :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}10\\cdot k+6\\cdot 5 \\\\ 25\\cdot k+15\\cdot 5\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}10k+30 \\\\ 25k+75\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}10k+30=0 \\\\ 25k+75=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to both of these equations is $k=-3.$"] [17592186366365 :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -14\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n\n- **II.** $\\lambda =-14$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n\n- **III.** The eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$"] [17592186366365 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,P-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nP-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -3 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda  & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -14-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(P-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4\\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -3 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda  & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -14-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -14-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3\\times 3$ determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-\\lambda )\\cdot (-14-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-14-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +14)\\lambda (\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $P$ are $\\lambda =2,$ $\\lambda =-2,$ $\\lambda =0,$ and $\\lambda =-14$  (all with algebraic multiplicity $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186366372 :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x & 2{y}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186366372 :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x & 2{y}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}],$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{y}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x=0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}=2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,1)$ and $(0,-1)$ are the stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x)=4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{y}^{2}-2)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}-2)=4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(H(0,1))=4\\cdot 4-{0}^{2}=16>0.\n$$\n\nNow, since $H(0,1)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,1)=({f}^{″}(0,1))_{11}=4>0,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(0,1).$\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,-1)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $H(0,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(H(-1,0))=4\\cdot (-4)-{0}^{2}=-16<0\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,-1)$ is saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,-1)$ and a local minimum at $(0,1).$"] [17592186366379 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$"] [17592186366379 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{5\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 3}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=6,$ $b=\\frac{9}{2},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{(6)}{(\\frac{9}{2})}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186366386 :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$"] [17592186366386 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1}{(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthonormal basis of $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11},$ $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11},$ $c=\\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11}|+|\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11}|+|\\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}|=\\frac{5\\sqrt{11}}{11}.\n$$"] [17592186366399 :question/problem "![](images/24/2489bb3ecac186e81ef421fdb90047e7a3d6a1ad5985bb0a8155857dca709996.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186366399 :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/4e/4e0e5c9cee037bded1f5739af35c3a8acfaa4d4e6f469b3e50afe69b96990e39.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/35/358dcedefd029cdbd07f3c64b1264e568bfbac134b92a607e9d55c632c277b13.png)"] [17592186366420 :question/problem "The basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]},\\,[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ a & b & c\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n$$\nT([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}x-z \\\\ x+y \\\\ x-y-z\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [17592186366420 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1},$ ${b}_{2},$ and ${b}_{3}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 8 & -2 & -2 & -8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 8 & -2 & -2 & -8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & 2 & -2 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{1}{4},$ $b=-\\frac{1}{4},$ $c=1$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=\\frac{1}{4}+(-\\frac{1}{4})+1=1.\n$$"] [17592186366441 :question/worked-solution "Any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2z \\\\ w \\\\ z \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]=z{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+w{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =4-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186366448 :question/worked-solution "Solving $2y-z=0$ for $z,$ we get $z=2y.$ Solving $x+y=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=-y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-y \\\\ y \\\\ 2y\\end{aligned}]=y{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186366455 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=y{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+z{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Solving $y-2z=0$ for $y,$ we get $y=2z.$ Solving $x+z=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=-z.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-z \\\\ 2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=z{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. If the columns of $T$ are such that ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}-{v}_{2},$ then the set of vectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent and therefore $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Now, according to the matrix invertible theorem, we obtain that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [17592186366476 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the orthogonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}].$"] [17592186366476 :question/worked-solution "The inverse of an orthogonal matrix is equal to its transpose.\n\nTherefore, since $D$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{-1} & ={D}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186366478 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366479 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & -\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366481 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366482 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366483 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the product $(2PQ)\\cdot (2PQ)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2PQ)\\cdot (2PQ)^{T} & =2PQ\\cdot 2{Q}^{T}{P}^{T} \\\\  & =4P(Q{Q}^{T}){P}^{T} \\\\  & =4P(I){P}^{T} \\\\  & =4I \\\\  & \\ne I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the following subtraction of two orthogonal matrices:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe resulting matrix is not orthogonal since, for example, its first column is not normalized.\n\n- Statement III is false. The inverse of an orthogonal matrix $P$ is given by ${P}^{-1}={P}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true\"."] [17592186366497 :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [17592186366497 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=3\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"] [17592186366504 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}14 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}30 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{45}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 14\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}30 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186366506 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366507 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366508 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366509 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366510 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366511 :question/problem "$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $D$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $D$\n\n- **II.** $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $D$\n\n- **III.** The eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$"] [17592186366511 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,D-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nD-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(D-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4\\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3\\times 3$ determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1-\\lambda )(-7-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(-7-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-2) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +7)({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +7)(\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}(\\lambda +7)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $D$ are $\\lambda =-1$ (with algebraic multiplicity $2$), $\\lambda =-7$ and $\\lambda =2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [17592186366518 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$"] [17592186366518 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,R-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nR-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(R-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st column, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(R-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -4)+2(-2\\lambda +7) & =0 \\\\ (-{\\lambda }^{3}+4{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -4)+(-4\\lambda +14) & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}+4{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186366525 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$"] [17592186366525 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{5}{3},$ $b=\\frac{1}{3},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{(\\frac{5}{3})}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=5.\n$$"] [17592186366553 :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0.5 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0.5 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186366553 :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\""] [17592186366560 :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186366560 :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{23}\\ne {q}_{32},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only.\""] [17592186366582 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366583 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366584 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366585 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -8\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366586 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186366587 :question/problem "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=1-3{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=-3t+{t}^{2},\\,q(t)=2-3t-5{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$"] [17592186366587 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=q(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(1-3{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(-3t+{t}^{2}) & =2-3t-5{t}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}t+(-3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =2-3t-5{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=2 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=2 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations, we get ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2+1=3.$"] [17592186366594 :question/problem "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=-{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=3{t}^{3}+3t+18,\\,{p}_{3}(t)={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+t-9,\\,\\,q(t)=-15{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-15t-9.\n$$\n\nIf $B={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}=$"] [17592186366594 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}(t)=q(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-t)+{x}_{2}(3{t}^{3}+3t+18)+{x}_{3}({t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+t-9) & =-15{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-15t-9 \\\\ (-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}){t}^{3}+(3{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}){t}^{2}+(-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3})t+(18{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3}) & =-15{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-15t-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-15 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-15 \\\\ 18{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3}=-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 1 & -15 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 18 & -9 & -9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 1 & -15 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 18 & -9 & -9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 1 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 4 & -47 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 18 & -9 & -9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+(-2){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 1 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 4 & -47 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -17 & 85\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{3}↔{R}_{4} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 1 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 4 & -47 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -17 & 85 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-15 \\\\ 9{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=-47 \\\\ -17{x}_{3}=85.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-5.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n9{x}_{2}+4\\cdot (-5)=-47\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the obtained values into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-{x}_{1}+3\\cdot (-3)+(-5)=-15\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],$ and therefore ${x}_{1}=1.$"] [17592186366601 :question/problem "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=-6{t}^{3}+6t+12,\\,{p}_{2}(t)={t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-3t-2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=-3{t}^{2}+2t,\\,\\,q(t)=-9{t}^{3}+19t+18.\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{2}=$"] [17592186366601 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}(t)=q(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-6{t}^{3}+6t+12)+{x}_{2}({t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-3t-2)+{x}_{3}(-3{t}^{2}+2t) & =-9{t}^{3}+19t+18 \\\\ (-6{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}){t}^{3}+(-2{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}){t}^{2}+(6{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3})t+(12{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}) & =-9{t}^{3}+19t+18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-9 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=19 \\\\ 12{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 2 & 19 \\\\ 12 & -2 & 0 & 18\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 2 & 19 \\\\ 12 & -2 & 0 & 18\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}